deep.reconstruction:
Folio 3v contains no mathematics by Dedekind. It is the blank side of a printed publisher's order form (Bestellzettel) that Dedekind reused as scrap paper. The sheet has been cut, so only the left half of the form survives.

Printed text visible:
- the heading 'Bestel[lzettel]';
- 'Herrn ....' in script type;
- 'Hiermit bestelle ich aus dem Verlage [...]' with the note '(Nichtgewünschtes gef[älligst durchstreichen])';
- 'WEIERSTRASS, Karl, Mathematisch[e Werke ... Vorlesungen über] die Theorie der Abelschen T[ranscendenten]';
- 'SCHERING, Ernst, Gesammelte Werk[e]';
- 'FÜRLE's Rechenblätter. Blatt I. II. [...]' and its '[...] Gebrauchserklä[rung]';
- 'Ort und Datum:' followed by a signature line.

These titles all belong to the list of the Berlin publisher Mayer & Müller, which issued Weierstrass's Mathematische Werke and Schering's Gesammelte Werke. The faint mirror-image formulas behind the print are show-through from the recto (folio 3r, the 'previous page'). Legible traces in mirror image include [r/a, s/b, 1] = 1/c, r/a = r'/c, [1, r/a] = a_1/a = 1/a_2, ∂/(a_2 b_2), γ^c = 1, sc' ≡ b_1 (mod b), c' ≡ c'_0 (mod b_2) and φ(c)/φ(b_2).

The recto's argument concerns the order of γ = α^r β^s in a product of cyclic groups (orders a and b) and counting residues c' mod c. It belongs to that page, not this one.

The only substantive information here is a dating clue, not a derivation. The Weierstrass volume on Abelian transcendents (Werke Bd. 4) and Schering's Gesammelte Werke (Bd. 1) both appeared in 1902. Assuming the notes postdate the form, this gives an approximate terminus post quem of 1902 for the mathematics on folio 3r (and plausibly for the bundle's surrounding sheets). That places the notes in Dedekind's late period.
